WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:11.963
Imagine a person choosing among three destinations. A box blocks one route. A helper robot can move the box, or simply carry the person to a destination itself.

00:00:12.383 --> 00:00:22.429
Both actions change the world. Only one clearly expands what the person can choose. That difference is the starting point for empowerment-based assistance.

00:00:23.179 --> 00:00:33.579
Move the box aside. Now the person's left, middle, and right choices lead to three distinct outcomes. Their action has more influence over what happens next.

00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:43.020
Carry them to the middle regardless of their choice, and that influence disappears. Even a neat-looking outcome can leave the human with less control.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:58.437
Learning to Assist Humans without Inferring Rewards explores assistance through human influence over future states. The assistant learns from interaction instead of first reconstructing an explicit human reward function.

00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:07.901
We will use the three routes to understand the objective, then examine how two learned predictors can estimate it, and where the interpretation needs care.

00:01:10.101 --> 00:01:18.985
Put the human's action at the input of our channel, and the future state at the output. The assistant changes the environment connecting those two.

00:01:19.405 --> 00:01:29.635
If knowing the human's action helps predict the outcome, there is mutual information. Clearing a route can increase that dependence by making another choice effective.

00:01:30.385 --> 00:01:39.856
But we do not generally choose how often the human presses each button. Their behavior supplies an action distribution, including habits and preferences.

00:01:40.276 --> 00:01:53.139
This matters mathematically. Information under an observed human policy differs from the maximum channel capacity obtained by optimizing an arbitrary distribution over human actions.

00:01:53.889 --> 00:02:05.781
For example, if the human always chooses left, observed action variation is absent. A perfectly usable middle route will not automatically reveal itself in those observations.

00:02:06.201 --> 00:02:17.403
The score depends on both the physical possibilities and the human behavior used to probe them. Keep that distinction when interpreting claims about increasing human empowerment.

00:02:19.603 --> 00:02:30.217
Suppose we could predict future outcomes twice. The first predictor knows the current context and the human's action; the second knows the context but not that action.

00:02:30.637 --> 00:02:40.385
If the action matters, the first predictor gains a real advantage. For a chosen left turn, its prediction concentrates on the left destination.

00:02:41.135 --> 00:02:51.374
The second predictor averages over the human's possible actions. Its prediction is broader because it has to account for left, middle, and right choices together.

00:02:51.794 --> 00:03:02.408
Compare the probability of the actual future under these two predictions. Their log ratio measures the extra information supplied by knowing the human's action.

00:03:03.158 --> 00:03:15.169
The paper learns the needed quantities using contrastive comparisons between observed futures and reference samples. The predictors distinguish compatible pairs from mismatched ones.

00:03:15.589 --> 00:03:27.740
We show probability bars to make the target understandable. The trained contrastive scores approximate density ratios rather than requiring an explicit table of every future state.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:40.744
A contrastive score contains more than the future probability we want. It compares that probability to a reference distribution used to draw contrasting examples.

00:03:41.164 --> 00:03:49.867
Picture the same gray reference term underneath both predictors. It is a shared measuring stick, rather than an extra source of human control.

00:03:50.617 --> 00:04:01.230
Subtract the action-blind score from the action-aware score. When their reference terms match, those terms cancel, leaving the log ratio between the two future predictions.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:13.790
Averaging the appropriate ratio over interactions gives the mutual-information target. Learning a good estimate still depends on data, model fit, and the assumptions of the estimator.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:26.218
This gives the assistant an intrinsic signal without explicitly identifying a human reward function first. It can favor changes that make the human's choices more consequential.

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:36.814
Return to the box: removing it separates the futures associated with different human actions. That separation is what the learning signal is trying to detect.

00:04:39.014 --> 00:04:48.441
Compare three worlds. With the box in place, two choices collapse onto the same accessible route. The human has fewer distinguishable effects.

00:04:48.861 --> 00:04:58.897
With the box removed, all three choices can lead to their intended destinations. In this toy example, the human's influence over the future increases.

00:04:59.647 --> 00:05:07.638
Now let the robot take over completely. It sends the human to the middle destination whether they choose left, middle, or right.

00:05:08.058 --> 00:05:19.247
The outcome may be consistent, but all three arrows merge. The human's action conveys no information about the destination, so this channel has zero mutual information.

00:05:19.997 --> 00:05:29.481
The picture illustrates why an assistant can be powerful while the person it assists is not. We must specify whose action supplies the channel input.

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:40.160
It also explains why task completion alone can miss a loss of agency. But preserving choice still does not tell us every preference the person has.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:52.974
A person might dislike all three destinations. Giving them more ways to choose among those destinations does not automatically make the available options desirable.

00:05:53.394 --> 00:06:05.169
Empowerment describes influence under a particular model of the interaction. Human benefit can depend on preferences, context, and outcomes that this measurement does not capture.

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:15.880
The paper relates its objective to reward under additional assumptions, including a model of human action choice and conditions on tasks and dynamics.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:27.916
Those assumptions carry real weight. The connection is not a universal proof that maximizing measured influence always improves every person's reward in every environment.

00:06:28.666 --> 00:06:37.540
Keep the image of clearing the blocked route. The assistant changes what is possible, while the human's choice still determines which future happens.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:47.842
That is the intuition. The two predictors provide a way to learn a signal for it, and the assumptions tell us how far that signal's interpretation can go.
